          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>On a non-contrast CT brain in the first two hours of ischaemic stroke, which fin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>On a non-contrast CT brain in the first two hours of ischaemic stroke, which finding indicates large-vessel occlusion and warrants urgent CT angiography?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Diffuse cerebral oedema with midline shift</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Diffuse oedema with midline shift suggests a large established infarct or space-occupying lesion, not an early sign of acute ischaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyperdense middle cerebral artery sign</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The hyperdense MCA sign represents a thrombus in the proximal middle cerebral artery, appearing as a bright segment in the Sylvian fissure. It may be the only CT abnormality in the first one to two hours.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bilateral basal ganglia calcification</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Basal ganglia calcification is a normal age-related finding unrelated to acute stroke.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Periventricular white-matter hypodensity</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Periventricular hypodensity suggests chronic small-vessel disease, not acute large-vessel occlusion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The hyperdense MCA sign — a bright segment in the Sylvian fissure representing a proximal MCA thrombus — indicates large-vessel occlusion and mandates urgent CT angiography, even if the rest of the scan appears normal."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The hyperdense MCA sign may be the only early abnormality. Always check the Sylvian fissure for asymmetric hyperdensity — this single finding warrants urgent CT angiography even when everything else looks normal."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
