          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 60-year-old man presents within three hours of acute right-sided weakness. Non</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 60-year-old man presents within three hours of acute right-sided weakness. Non-contrast CT brain shows no haemorrhage. CT angiography reveals proximal left middle cerebral artery occlusion. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Observe and repeat CT brain after 24 hours</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Observation wastes critical time. The ischaemic penumbra shrinks every minute, and both alteplase and thrombectomy are indicated in this scenario.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Administer intravenous alteplase and plan mechanical thrombectomy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Within the 4.5-hour window with no haemorrhage, intravenous alteplase is indicated. The proximal MCA occlusion on CTA additionally qualifies the patient for mechanical thrombectomy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start therapeutic anticoagulation with heparin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Anticoagulation alone is not the treatment for acute ischaemic stroke with large-vessel occlusion. Thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy are the evidence-based acute interventions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urgent neurosurgical decompression</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Decompressive surgery may be considered later for malignant MCA infarction with cerebral oedema, but it is not the immediate management step in acute ischaemic stroke.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When NCCT excludes haemorrhage within 4.5 hours, administer intravenous alteplase. When CTA confirms proximal MCA occlusion, plan mechanical thrombectomy — the window for thrombectomy can extend to 24 hours if salvageable tissue remains on CT perfusion."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "No haemorrhage clears the path for alteplase, and the CTA-confirmed large-vessel occlusion adds thrombectomy — a powerful image-to-action chain that the video builds across scenes."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
