          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Across the four conditions covered in this video, which single imaging finding p</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Across the four conditions covered in this video, which single imaging finding produces the most dramatic reversal of the initial management plan?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cardiomegaly on chest X-ray in suspected heart failure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cardiomegaly supports the diagnosis of heart failure but does not reverse the management plan — diuresis and further assessment proceed regardless.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemorrhage on non-contrast CT brain in acute stroke</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Haemorrhage on NCCT fundamentally reverses stroke management from thrombolysis to blood-pressure control and anticoagulation reversal. Giving thrombolysis into a haemorrhage is lethal — this single finding flips the entire treatment approach.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatorenal sign on abdominal ultrasound</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The hepatorenal sign confirms fatty infiltration and prompts lifestyle modification, but it is an expected finding that does not reverse the management direction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bilateral pleural effusions on chest X-ray</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pleural effusions indicate advanced congestion and guide diuretic intensity, but the management shift is incremental — it does not reverse the treatment direction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Haemorrhage on NCCT produces the most dramatic management reversal. Both stroke types look identical at the bedside, yet their treatments are opposite — thrombolysis saves ischaemic brain but is lethal in haemorrhage."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The presence or absence of haemorrhage on NCCT is the single most consequential imaging finding in acute medicine — it determines whether the patient receives thrombolysis or the opposite intervention."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
